{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4bzARAmcNenf7HDYh8C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KJ4RRWbneqULyaeHbASJU8","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"report":"PAYLOAD 24/32 — files reviewed against the redacted candidate tree: .claude/scripts/audit-query.py, audit-verify-chain.py, audit_spool_state.py, budget-summary.py, and the new caller_repo_guard.py. I also read the modules they now delegate to: .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_query/* and .claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/*.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS TOUCHED BY THIS PAYLOAD\n- Condition 3(c) is TRUE for this payload. audit_spool_state.py:62 sets sys.dont_write_bytecode = True before the sys.path walk and before `from _lib import runtime_paths` (lines 64-69). Its only non-test importer, check-audit-real-loss.py, does the same at :67, before `import audit_spool_state` at :71. No hook imports audit_spool_state, so the process-wide flag cannot leak into a hook.\n- Condition 7 matches the code. audit-query.py and budget-summary.py keep their paths and CLI entry points, and the logic now lives in _lib/_audit_query/ and _lib/_budget_summary_parts/. Neither directory is on the condition-5 kernel list, which is correct because neither is a governance hook.\n- CHANGELOG honesty. \"Fixed — audit-verify-chain.py --strict-against-counter\" (CHANGELOG.md:300-308) matches the code, including \"Without the flag, nothing changes\": main() at audit-verify-chain.py:826-849 sends non-strict runs through the same _resolve_log_path, _read_key, _iter_lines and verify() as before. The removed strict_against_counter/counter_override arguments were always False/None on that path, and a bad argument still gets stock argparse's rc 2 because strict_json stays None. The installer note that caller_repo_guard.py reaches installs (CHANGELOG.md:342-346) is accurate.\n\nADOPTER BLAST RADIUS\n- No registered hook, settings file or session-start path calls any of these five scripts; every hook mention is in a comment. Nothing here can disable governance, change model or effort, or block a session.\n- Fresh install: install.sh:1520-1549 copies every top-level _lib entry, subdirectories included, so both new subpackages ship. Upgrade baseline: _framework_manifest_set.sh:179 and :257-292 walk .claude/hooks recursively. upgrade.sh itself is withheld from my copy, so I could check only the shared enumerator, not upgrade.sh's own code.\n- MCP read path (mcp-server/handlers/audit_query.py:109-140) loads the facade by path. If the import fails it degrades to an `import_failed:` warning (fails open). All 27 allowlisted cmd_* names still resolve: 22 thin wrappers, cmd_benchmarks still defined in place, and 4 rebound functions.\n\nMOVED CODE (import order, global lookup, patch points)\n- audit-query.py:177-232. The five declaration modules' __all__ values are deep-copied into the facade. Then every function from all eight modules is cloned with the facade's globals(), so name lookups and test monkeypatches keep their old behavior. The wrappers at :235-245 and :360-370 override the clone names, and nested calls (for example cmd_health → cmd_compliance) go through the wrapper.\n- Write location of the label store: audit-query.py:203 re-pins _LABEL_STORE_PATH to .claude/scripts/audit-log-labels.jsonl, the same as the base. The module's own value (an _audit_query/ sibling path) is never used. The sys.path insert in _compute_label_hmac (labeling.py:51) resolves through the facade's __file__ to .claude/hooks/_lib, as before. No write moves.\n- No name collisions between extracted functions and the facade's own defs (default_log_path, default_errors_path, discover_logs, read_entries). Every global the extracted code uses (argparse, csv, io, json, os, re, sys, Counter, defaultdict, datetime, timedelta, timezone, Path, typing names) is imported by the facade. check-audit-read-api-stable.py still finds every frozen public def.\n- budget-summary.py:371-397 uses the same rebinding. The part modules contain only defs: annotations are lazy and parameter defaults are self-contained (native_reconciliation.py:3-6), so they import cleanly on 3.9. The facade's _load_normalize_model_name (:400) supersedes the cost_rollup copy and has an equivalent body. _NORMALIZE_MODEL_NAME, _TX_MODULE and SOURCE_CHOICES/AUDIT_BANNER/ROOT_ENV are resolved at import as before (:425-429), and build_parser reads them at call time. I spot-checked moved bodies (audit_events.py in full, presentation_cli.main): text identical.\n- Python: standard library only and 3.9-compatible in all five files (FunctionType 5-argument form, string annotations, no 3.10+ syntax).\n\nFAIL DIRECTION\n- The strict verifier fails closed with named reasons and never a traceback (audit-verify-chain.py:630-673), which is right for a verifier CLI. The counter format the writer produces (audit_hmac.py:646, str(n)) satisfies _COUNTER_RE, so a legitimately written counter is never rejected. FIFOs are refused by fstat on the same descriptor (:545-570). The CI F6 gate (validate.yml:685-715) uses only the non-strict path.\n\nP0: none.\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): none. No undeclared P1 in this payload.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. P2 — .claude/scripts/audit-verify-chain.py:588-591. The strict counter check reads only the first 64 bytes. A counter file of \"5\" followed by 63+ bytes of whitespace and then trailing garbage is accepted as 5, although the docstring says a counter that is \"not plain decimal digits\" exits rc 3. The writer never produces this. Minimal fix: read 65 bytes and fail if more than 64 come back, or read the whole regular file with a size cap.\n2. P2 — audit-verify-chain.py:579-587 versus :617-626 and CHANGELOG.md:305. When permissions refuse the lstat of the counter (parent directory not searchable), the result is rc 3 chain_length_unreadable. The same refusal on the log falls through to the open and gives rc 4. The docstring promises rc 4 \"when the read is refused by permissions\", while the CHANGELOG just says \"unreadable counter exits 3\". Minimal fix: map PermissionError from lstat to EXIT_PERM in _expected_length_strict and make the CHANGELOG line say \"3 (4 when refused by permissions)\".\n3. P2 — audit-verify-chain.py:642-655. In strict mode with stdin, stream.close() also closes sys.stdin.buffer. An in-process caller of main([...,'--stdin','--strict-against-counter']) loses stdin afterwards; the CLI itself is unaffected. Minimal fix: call stream.detach() instead of close() when log_path is None.\n4. P2 — .claude/scripts/caller_repo_guard.py:52-53. The scrub removes only GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE, but the comment says no repo-redirecting environment may decide the answer. GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY, GIT_INDEX_FILE and GIT_NAMESPACE still pass through, so a harness run from inside a git hook could fingerprint the wrong refs. This is a maintainer tool, inert on installs. Minimal fix: drop every variable in `git rev-parse --local-env-vars`, or every GIT_* except an allowlist.\n5. P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/*.py and _audit_query/queries.py:330. The extracted modules resolve names only through the facade's globals: the parts have no imports, and cmd_errors calls the facade-only default_errors_path. Importing and calling them directly raises NameError, while condition 7 tells hand-patchers to patch \"the module\". Patching the file works through the facade. Minimal fix: a one-line header in each part saying it is facade-bound and must not be called directly. Optionally add an __init__.py to _budget_summary_parts for parity with _audit_query.","decision":"GO","sentence":"In this payload every declared condition it touches (3(c), 7 and the matching CHANGELOG lines) is true against the code, the moved audit-query and budget-summary logic keeps its name lookup, patch points and write locations, nothing here reaches a registered hook, and the only findings are five P2 follow-ups with no P0 and no undeclared P1."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4093,"cache_read_input_tokens":246538,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4093},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"208d6398-50f3-4598-a736-f21d26865a99","uuid":"97b922f2-9376-41b8-a9b4-84f0ab966c2f","timestamp":"2026-11-15T11:05:33.771Z","request_id":"req_011Ch4bzAAHDwvvDqiZnyihT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KJ4RRWbneqULyaeHbASJU8":{"report":"PAYLOAD 24/32 — files reviewed against the redacted candidate tree: .claude/scripts/audit-query.py, audit-verify-chain.py, audit_spool_state.py, budget-summary.py, and the new caller_repo_guard.py. I also read the modules they now delegate to: .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_query/* and .claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/*.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS TOUCHED BY THIS PAYLOAD\n- Condition 3(c) is TRUE for this payload. audit_spool_state.py:62 sets sys.dont_write_bytecode = True before the sys.path walk and before `from _lib import runtime_paths` (lines 64-69). Its only non-test importer, check-audit-real-loss.py, does the same at :67, before `import audit_spool_state` at :71. No hook imports audit_spool_state, so the process-wide flag cannot leak into a hook.\n- Condition 7 matches the code. audit-query.py and budget-summary.py keep their paths and CLI entry points, and the logic now lives in _lib/_audit_query/ and _lib/_budget_summary_parts/. Neither directory is on the condition-5 kernel list, which is correct because neither is a governance hook.\n- CHANGELOG honesty. \"Fixed — audit-verify-chain.py --strict-against-counter\" (CHANGELOG.md:300-308) matches the code, including \"Without the flag, nothing changes\": main() at audit-verify-chain.py:826-849 